(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-2-chronic-vision-loss-quiz"]={"title":"Chronic Vision Loss Quiz 1 — CMS I Exam 2","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 69-year-old man with early macular degeneration still smokes. Which instruction matters most?","choices":["Avoid all screen time","Stop smoking","Avoid reading in dim light","Limit dietary salt"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It is the modifiable risk factor that counts. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A patient asks whether glaucoma treatment will restore lost vision. What is true?","choices":["Vision returns once pressure is normal","Damage already done cannot be reversed","Drops restore the lost field over months","Surgery reverses optic nerve cupping"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, though treatment preserves what remains. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 57-year-old woman has a pigmented iris lesion. Which feature most suggests melanoma over a freckle?","choices":["A prominent feeder vessel","Brown colour","Presence since puberty","A flat surface"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Tumours build their own blood supply; freckles do not. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 26-year-old woman with raised intracranial pressure develops horizontal diplopia. Which nerve is affected?","choices":["Facial, the seventh","Optic, the second","Abducens, the sixth","Trochlear, the fourth"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It is the classic false localising sign here. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"An 84-year-old man's dry macular degeneration turns wet. What has changed?","choices":["New vessels from the choroid bleed into retina","Aqueous humour leaks into the lens","Vitreous separates from the retina","The lens proteins clump"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and the blood organises into a scar. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 33-year-old woman asks what will actually resolve her idiopathic intracranial hypertension. What is the answer?","choices":["Lifelong acetazolamide","A ventriculoperitoneal shunt","Optic nerve sheath fenestration","Weight reduction"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Medical therapy covers the patient while that happens. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A 5-year-old boy has reduced vision in one eye that glasses do not correct. What is the commonest cause of childhood vision loss?","choices":["Congenital cataract","Glaucoma","Macular degeneration","Amblyopia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, affecting 3 to 5 percent of children. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Parents of a child with treated retinoblastoma ask about outcome. What is true?","choices":["It is always fatal","Treatment only preserves vision","Recurrence is expected lifelong","Over 95 percent five-year survival when treated"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, against near-certain death untreated. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 77-year-old man asks what will fix his cataract. What is the answer?","choices":["Stronger glasses indefinitely","Laser to reshape the cornea","Anti-VEGF injections","Surgery to replace the lens"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, deferred until it interferes with daily activities. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A patient has optic nerve pallor rather than cupping. Which diagnosis does that favour?","choices":["Cataract","Astigmatism","Ocular ischaemic syndrome","Primary open-angle glaucoma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and carotid stenosis underlies it. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman starts drops for glaucoma. How do they lower the pressure?","choices":["By thinning the cornea","By constricting retinal arterioles","By reducing vitreous volume","Less aqueous made, or more drained"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Every class works on one side or the other. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman has a flat 2 mm pigmented spot on the inferior iris, unchanged for years. What is the plan?","choices":["Refer urgently for enucleation","Biopsy it in clinic","Refer to ophthalmology for surveillance","Reassure with no follow-up"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It is likely benign, but growth has to be excluded. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 7-year-old girl reads 20/50 with one eye and 20/30 with the other. What defines unilateral amblyopia?","choices":["Two or more lines of difference","Any difference at all","Worse than 20/200 in one eye","Loss of colour vision"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, on best corrected acuity. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 40-year-old man reports double vision that disappears when he covers either eye. What does that indicate?","choices":["Binocular diplopia from ocular misalignment","A cataract in one eye","Macular degeneration","Amblyopia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Covering one eye removes the second image. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman has new headache and papilledema. Which study comes first?","choices":["MRI of the brain with MR venography","Lumbar puncture straight away","Non-contrast CT of the head","Fluorescein angiography"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A mass and a venous sinus thrombosis both have to be excluded first. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"An adult treated late for childhood amblyopia has good acuity but struggles with 3D films. Why?","choices":["He has developed glaucoma","Stereo vision never developed","The lens is still cloudy","He has an uncorrected refractive error"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The brain still uses one eye at a time. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 46-year-old man on long-term corticosteroids has glare, trouble reading, and symptoms that ease once dilated. Which cataract is this?","choices":["Congenital polar","Zonular cataract","Posterior subcapsular","Nuclear cataract"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It comes on faster and classically under fifty, with steroids or diabetes. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 3-year-old girl has one eye with a much stronger prescription than the other. How does amblyopia arise?","choices":["The lens clouds over","The retina detaches","The brain favours one eye and the other fails to develop","The optic nerve is compressed"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Strabismus, refractive difference or deprivation all do this. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"An 82-year-old man is told he has the dry form. What share of macular degeneration is dry?","choices":["About 95 percent","It varies with no usual split","About 80 percent","About 20 percent"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, leaving roughly 20 percent wet. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 3-year-old has a retinal mass suspicious for retinoblastoma. Why is biopsy avoided?","choices":["It would damage the optic nerve","It risks seeding tumour cells","The tissue is too small to sample","Anaesthesia is unsafe at that age"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, so diagnosis rests on examination and imaging. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A patient is nearsighted. Where do distant images focus?","choices":["Behind the retina","At several points at once","Directly on the retina","In front of the retina"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The eye has too much refractive power. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 24-year-old woman has blur at every distance that glasses correct. Why does astigmatism do this?","choices":["Uneven curvature gives several focal points","The eyeball is too short","The lens has hardened with age","The lens has gone cloudy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, so no single point focus forms. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"A 9-year-old boy with Down syndrome is screened for eye problems. Which is he more likely to have?","choices":["Conjunctival melanoma","Esotropia","Uveal melanoma","Presbyopia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Down syndrome carries a higher prevalence of it. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A newborn has a parent who had retinoblastoma. When should the baby be seen?","choices":["Only if the red reflex is abnormal","Only if vision seems poor","Within the first eight weeks of life","At the one-year check"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, by someone experienced in retinoblastoma. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman's vision clouds again two years after cataract surgery. What has happened, and what treats it?","choices":["Glaucoma has developed","Posterior capsule opacification, treated by laser","The cataract has regrown","Macular degeneration has started"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Residual lens epithelial cells cloud the capsule. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 2-year-old boy is calm when his left eye is covered but cries when the right is covered. What does that show?","choices":["He has retinoblastoma","The left eye is amblyopic","The right eye is amblyopic","Both eyes see equally"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Losing the good eye is what distresses him. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Which patient most needs a yearly ophthalmologist rather than an optometrist?","choices":["A patient with a stye that has settled","A patient with diabetes","A student needing a new prescription","A patient wanting contact lens fitting"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A medical eye problem needs the medical specialist. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"An 80-year-old woman reports a blur in the centre of her vision. Which condition fits?","choices":["Macular degeneration, a central scotoma","Cataract","Retinal detachment","Amblyopia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The macula serves central vision. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man needs treatment for choroidal melanoma. Which is used most often now?","choices":["Enucleation in every case","Topical chemotherapy drops","Observation alone","Radiation therapy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and it spares the globe where removal once did not. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"A newborn is found to have a cataract. Which paediatric type is commonest?","choices":["Zonular","Polar","Posterior lenticonus","Cortical"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, a white opacity round the nucleus. — Chronic Vision Loss & Tumors - Dr Rappa.pptx, Slide 38"}]};
